::page{title="Get started" description="Open a site by its key, tabs, the desktop, install or bookmark." order=1}

# Get started

::toc

## Get HyperBrowser

Download it from [the releases page](https://github.com/tibocub/HyperBrowser/releases/latest). There is no installer to run.

- **Windows**: `HyperBrowser.zip`. Extract it (right-click, **Extract All…**) and put the `HyperBrowser` folder where it can stay (`Documents` for example). Double-click `HyperBrowser.exe` in it. Windows says "Windows protected your PC" because the files aren't signed: click **More info**, then **Run anyway**. The first start of a new version takes longer.
- **Linux**: `HyperBrowser.AppImage`. Put it where it can stay, make it executable (`chmod +x HyperBrowser.AppImage`) and run it. On Ubuntu 22.04 or newer, AppImages need `libfuse2` (`sudo apt install libfuse2`).

HyperBrowser updates itself, peer to peer: a new version downloads while it runs and starts the next time you open it. To start it right away, choose **Update ready: restart** in **HyperBrowser ▾**. Your identity, your sites and your settings stay as they are.

To run it from its [source code](https://github.com/tibocub/HyperBrowser) instead (Node.js 22 or newer, git): put `hypergraph`, `SwarmFS` and `HyperBrowser` side by side, run `npm install` in each (hypergraph and SwarmFS first), then `npm start` in `HyperBrowser`.

## The window

HyperBrowser draws its own window. At the top:

- **HyperBrowser ▾**: your identity (and your other identities, if you made some), your installed sites, open a site by key, your devices, the look (system, light or dark, and the accent colour), and what closing the window does.
- **The tabs**, one per open site. The desktop is always the first tab. `+` opens a new one.
- Under them, **the toolbar**: back, forward, reload, the address, the bookmark button ☆, and the site's state.

A site only ever draws below these bars. Anything above them is HyperBrowser itself.

## Closing the window

Closing the window doesn't stop HyperBrowser: it stays in the tray (by the clock) so your computer keeps hosting your installed sites, your own sites and the forums you joined. Right-click its icon to open the window again, see how many sites it hosts, or **Quit**.

Rather have it quit? In **HyperBrowser ▾**, under "When the window closes", choose **Quit**.

On Linux, the tray depends on your desktop (GNOME needs the AppIndicator extension). Without one, closing the window quits.

## Open a site by its key

A site's key is 52 letters and digits. Paste it in the address and press Enter. The address then shows the site's name and a short key, like `HyperBrowser · 6x99a3…cjy`; click it to see the whole key.

If nobody who has the site is online, the tab says so: try again later.

## Tabs

Each site opens in its own tab, and keeps running when you switch to another one.

| Keys | |
|---|---|
| Ctrl+T | the desktop, address ready |
| Ctrl+W | close the tab |
| Ctrl+Tab, Ctrl+Shift+Tab | next, previous tab |
| Ctrl+L | the address |
| F5, Ctrl+R | reload |
| Alt+Left, Alt+Right | back, forward |

## The desktop

The desktop is your home: each site's icon and name. Double-click an icon (or press Enter) to open it. Right-click it to install it, make it a bookmark, or remove it. Drag icons to arrange them.

The official site (this one), the official forum, Apps, Chat and Contacts are already there.

## Get more apps

Open **Apps** on the desktop: HyperBrowser's apps, each with a line about it. Click **Install**: HyperBrowser asks "Install …?" with the app's name. Say yes and it's on your desktop, installed. Already installed? The button says **Open**.

New apps appear in Apps as they are published: no need to update HyperBrowser. Any site can offer an Install button the same way, and HyperBrowser always asks you first.

## Use it on two computers

Your PC and your laptop can be **one identity**: the same you everywhere, the same forums and roles, the same installed sites.

1. On the computer you already use: **HyperBrowser ▾** → **Devices…** → **Add a device**. Copy the code and send it to yourself.
2. On the other computer: **HyperBrowser ▾** → **Devices…** → **Join with a code**, paste it, **Join**.

The second computer's own identity is set aside (kept, not deleted). Lost a computer? **Remove** it from the Devices window of another one. Write down your **recovery phrase** (Devices → Recovery phrase): it is your identity.

## Identities: keep parts of your life apart

An **identity** is a separate you: its own key, contacts, conversations, circles, forums, desktop and site data. People who meet one of your identities can't tell from HyperBrowser that the others are you. Your first one is called **Main**.

- **New identity…**: **HyperBrowser ▾** → **New identity…**, give it a name (only you see it), **Create**.
- **Switch**: **HyperBrowser ▾** → **Switch to …**. The window closes and opens again under the other identity: one is open at a time, and nothing of the other keeps running.
- **Manage identities…**: rename one, show its **recovery phrase**, or delete one you don't use. Write each phrase down: it is that identity.
- **Delete…** removes everything of that identity from this computer, for good. HyperBrowser shows its recovery phrase first. Keep the phrase: it is the only way to get that identity back, and even then not what this computer kept for it.
- **Restore from a recovery phrase…**: in **Manage identities…**, give the identity a name, type its 24 words, **Restore**. This computer becomes a new device of that identity: your forums, roles and settings come back from your other devices and circles as they are reached. If the words are wrong, nothing is added and HyperBrowser says so.

What identities don't do yet, honestly:

- **Same IP address.** Every regular identity connects from your internet connection. Someone who sees two of your identities online from one address may guess they are one person. An **anonymous** identity doesn't show it (below).
- **Not hidden on this computer.** Anyone who uses your computer can see your identities and their recovery phrases. A protected vault comes later.
- **You are still you.** People you meet under two identities may recognise how you write, when you are online, or who your friends are.

## Anonymous identities: hide your IP

Everyone you read or post with sees your IP address next to what you do: HyperBrowser has no servers, so people's computers talk to each other. An **anonymous** identity talks only through **I2P**, a network where your traffic hops through other people's computers first, so nobody you meet learns where you are.

- **Make one**: **Manage identities…**, tick **Anonymous**, give it a name, **Create**. It stays anonymous for good: an identity can't be changed into one, or back.
- **Opening it** says **Connecting to I2P…** until I2P is ready (tens of seconds, sometimes minutes). Nothing of it goes online before, and it never falls back to your regular connection: if I2P can't be reached, it waits and says why.
- **Slower.** Fine for chat and forums; a big file (photos, videos) takes minutes. HyperBrowser tells you when one will.
- **Photos lose their hidden details.** A photo you share loses its camera, time and place data first; your own file stays as it is. Photos in a shared folder aren't shared as they are: HyperBrowser offers to add them one by one, without those details.
- **One device only.** An anonymous identity can't be added to a second computer (that needs the regular network); you can still restore it from its recovery phrase. It also doesn't keep copies of your circles' forums for them.
- **Not findable by default.** Being findable (so people can send you a friend request by your key) tells anyone who knows that key when you are online. Turn it on in Contacts if you want it.
- **Updates pause** while it is open: they come over the regular network.
- **Switching**: opening an anonymous identity right after a regular one closed (or the other way), HyperBrowser warns you: someone watching both could link them by the timing. Waiting 10 minutes between them is safer.

What it protects you from: the people you meet, site owners, your internet provider and anyone on your local network, who see you use I2P but not what you do or who you are. What it doesn't: someone watching the whole internet at once, or a state set on finding one person (by comparing timings); and what you post yourself (your name, how you write, a face in a photo) still tells.

**HyperBrowser relays for I2P.** Your computer passes along other people's I2P traffic, unseen and capped at 256 KB/s: that is what keeps I2P working for everyone. It opens its port on your home router (UPnP) to do so. Both can be turned off in **Configuration**; in some countries, where relaying I2P is risky, it is off by itself. On a phone's connection (a hotspot) your computer can't be reached and relays little.

## Install or bookmark

There is only one kind of thing in HyperBrowser: a site. You keep it in one of two ways:

- **Installed** (solid frame): every page and file of the site is kept on your computer and never thrown away. Your computer serves it to others, even when the site isn't open.
- **Bookmark** (dotted frame): an icon to open the site quickly. Its pages are kept for a while, then thrown away to make room. Your computer serves it to others while it's open, and for a short time after you close it.

The ☆ button in the toolbar adds or removes a bookmark.

## What "hosted" means

There is no server. A site is **hosted** by every computer that has it and is online: its owner's, and its visitors'. The more people keep a site, the easier it is to reach.

The right of the toolbar shows the site's state:

- `■ complete` or `□ loading`: whether your computer has the whole site
- how many **peers** (other computers with the site) you are connected to
- how much of the site your computer holds, and `kept` when the site is installed

If the owner is offline and nobody else who has the site is online, the site can't be reached until someone comes back.
